    "final_message": "I've sent the report to the caller. The red acceptance test is written and committed (9011f2c), and it fails at the user story's \"then\".\n\n`scripts/verify story-drafting` exits 1. It gets through tessercheck with zero findings, `mypy --strict` and the non-root tests, then serves the host on port 9370. The failure comes in the root tier. Submitting a JTBD through the host succeeds, and reading it back returns the same `jtbd_id`. But the read has no `step`, so this is the failure:\n\n```\nAssertionError: assert ('88cf4023-9d...87b26e', None) == ('88cf4023-9d...', 'drafting')\nAt index 1 diff: None != 'drafting'\n```\n\n- **Test:** `TestSubmittingAJtbd.test_a_just_submitted_jtbd_is_drafting` in `<repo>/story-drafting/tests/test_acceptance.py`. It submits with `POST /jtbds`, then checks with `GET /jtbds/{jtbd_id}`, retrying for up to 30 seconds until the read answers. It then asserts that the answer has `\"step\": \"drafting\"`. I kept the generated test except for its last assertion, which checked that `job` was read back.\n- **Spec:** context `story_drafting` with a `Jtbd` aggregate identified by `jtbd_id`. Its only field is `job`, with no status field, because a submitted JTBD has no status yet. The client operations are `submit_jtbd` and `check_status`. The workflow runs `work_through_jtbd` in `start` mode, because this workflow will later wait for the developer's pick. It runs one action, `record_submission`, and saves to the `jtbds` store in memory, served over HTTP.\n\nFiles are in `<repo>/.baseline-runs/red-v9-opus-low/`:\n- `spec.toml`\n- `verify.log`"
  },
